EXT. SALT FLAT STAGE STOP - NOON

White to the horizon. Heat bending the air. A lone shack. A dead coach.

HATTIE MORROW, 40s, hat low, holds a rifle. BRODIE, 30s, a hired gun, has a revolver pointed at her. LAZARO, 20s, the driver, crouches by a wheel. SNOW, an old white-whiskered prospector, watches from the shack's shade.

HATTIE
(to Brodie)
You won't shoot a woman in the back.

BRODIE
You're facing me.

HATTIE
Then you won't shoot a woman in the front.

A pause. The wind.

LAZARO
(to everyone)
Please. Nobody has to die today.

SNOW
(muttering)
Everybody has to die someday.

BRODIE
Shut up, old man.

Brodie's eyes flick to Snow. Hattie's rifle snaps up. A shot rings. Brodie spins, drops his revolver, grabs his shoulder.

Silence. Smoke drifts across the flats.

HATTIE
(to Lazaro)
Get the water.

LAZARO
Yes, ma'am.

SNOW
(to Brodie, gently)
Told you.
